# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so
.Python
# Build artefacts are scoped to the Python tree on purpose. A bare `lib/` used to
# match frontend/src/lib as well, which silently kept fifteen frontend source
# files out of the repository and broke the published build. Keep these rules
# anchored so they cannot swallow source directories elsewhere.
backend/build/
backend/develop-eggs/
backend/dist/
backend/downloads/
backend/eggs/
backend/.eggs/
backend/lib/
backend/lib64/
backend/parts/
backend/sdist/
backend/var/
backend/wheels/
backend/*.egg-info/
/.installed.cfg
*.egg
poetry.lock

# Virtual Environment
venv/
env/
.venv/
# Any interpreter directory whose name begins with `.venv` — `.venv313`,
# `.venv-alerts`, `.venv-verify`. `scripts/check_tracked_sources.py` fails the
# build when an ignore rule hides source, and it failed here for the opposite
# reason: a developer's local environment under a name these rules did not know
# was reported as an unignored file inside the checkout, so a red `repo-hygiene`
# job had nothing to do with the change being tested. One glob covers the family.
**/.venv*/
.env
.env.*
*.env
!.env.example
!.env.*.example
backend/.deps/

# Local/internal files and secrets
.freebuff/
AGENTS.md
**/AGENTS.md
.secrets/
secrets/
credentials/
*.pem
*.key
*.crt
*.p12
*.pfx
*.secret
*.credentials

# Internal documentation (audit records, remediation plans, internal state).
# These are reproducible records for the team that owns the repository, not
# committed product docs. Put anything you would not publish in the handbook here.
docs/internal/

# Celery Beat persistent schedule
celerybeat-schedule
celerybeat-schedule*

# Database / Storage
*.db
*.sqlite
file:*
_tmp_*
*.tmp
*.bak
*.orig
reports_store/*
!reports_store/.gitkeep
*.pdf

# Backup artefacts (`make backup`). Dumps contain the full asset inventory,
# findings and audit trail in the clear, so they must never be committed — and
# they must live somewhere the operator can copy offsite.
backups/
*.dump
reports-*.tgz

# Exported audit streams (`verify_audit_chain --ndjson`). A newline-delimited
# export of the audit trail is the complete record of who did what, from which
# address and when, with the hashes that make it verifiable — the single most
# sensitive file this platform can write to disk, and the easiest to commit by
# accident, because the documented workflow is "export it, then check it".
*.ndjson

# Node / Frontend
frontend/node_modules/
frontend/dist/
frontend/dist-ssr/
frontend/.vite/
*.tsbuildinfo
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
pnpm-debug.log*
*.local

# Docker volumes and local overrides
postgres_data/
redis_data/
volumes/
docker-compose.override.yml
docker-compose.local.yml

# IDE / OS
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Alembic
alembic/versions/*.pyc
alembic/versions/__pycache__/

# Coverage / Testing
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
# `npm run test:coverage` — the frontend job in CI — writes its HTML and lcov
# report here. The `frontend/dist/` entries above cover the build output, not the
# report, so without this line a coverage run leaves a directory that a broad
# `git add` would commit.
frontend/coverage/
# Written by the critical-coverage gate (Makefile + CI) and by the coverage
# program's own full runs, never committed.
coverage-critical.json
coverage.json
htmlcov/
.tox/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/

# Logs
logs/
*.log

# Accidental Windows device-name files
nul
**/nul

# Windows copy/corrupt artefacts (failed copy operations leave empty dirs with ;X suffix)
# Example: .env.example;C, README.md;C — these are empty directories from interrupted copies
*;C
**/*;C
